<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frostating</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Frostathing</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Frostating was the medieval law assembly for Trøndelag in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, meeting on the Frosta peninsula in the Trondheimsfjord. Its law code, the Frostating Law, traditionally associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-the-good">Haakon the Good</a>, is one of the main sources for early Norwegian law. It served the region until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-vi-of-norway">Magnus the Lawmender</a>'s national code of 1274, and the name survives in the Frostating Court of Appeal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frostating.jpg" alt="Frostating" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Toreau at Norwegian Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tinghaugen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
